  "worked_solution": "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t+1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\int (t+1)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{(t+1)^{1/2}}{1/2}+C \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{t+1}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=-6.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6 & =6\\sqrt{1}+C \\\\ -6 & =6+C \\\\ C & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by \n\n$$\nv(t)=6\\sqrt{t+1}-12.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (6\\sqrt{t+1}-12)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\int (t+1)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}t-12\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{(t+1)^{3/2}}{3/2}-12t+K \\\\  & =4(t+1)^{3/2}-12t+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(0)=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =4(1)^{3/2}-0+K \\\\ 4 & =4+K \\\\ K & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=4(t+1)^{3/2}-12t.\n$$",
